<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<div class="callout" id="at-a-glance"><p><strong>Dead hang at a glance</strong></p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>What it is:</strong> a static hold from a bar with straight arms. Your grip carries your whole bodyweight.</li>
          <li><strong>How long:</strong> a common start is 2 to 4 holds of 10 to 20 seconds with 60 to 90 seconds rest, 3 days a week. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</li>
          <li><strong>What it trains:</strong> mainly forearm grip endurance. Shoulders, lats and trunk assist.</li>
          <li><strong>Check first:</strong> sharp pain, a recent shoulder injury or an unstable shoulder means ask a clinician before you hang.</li>
        </ol></div>
        <div class="cta-actions">
          <a href="/dead-hang-time-by-age/" class="cta-btn">How long to hang, by age</a>
          <a href="/deadhang-benefits/" class="cta-btn">Dead hang benefits</a>
          <a href="/how-to-deadhang/" class="cta-btn">How to dead hang: form</a>
          <a href="/beginner-deadhang/" class="cta-btn">Cannot hang yet?</a>
        </div>

        <h2>What Is a Dead Hang?</h2>
        <p>A dead hang is a static hold from a pull-up bar with arms fully extended and bodyweight supported entirely by your grip. You grab the bar, step off the ground and hang. No pulling and no swinging.</p>
        <p>A <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">passive dead hang</a> lets the shoulders relax toward the ears. An active dead hang pulls the shoulder blades down and back with the arms still straight. Both train grip endurance, and many programs alternate between them.</p>

        <h2>Dead Hang Benefits at a Glance</h2>
        <p>People hang for grip endurance, shoulder and spine stretching, and pull-up preparation. Only the link between grip strength and longevity has cohort evidence, and grip is a marker and not a cause. The rest is common practice, with no trial showing lasting change.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/deadhang-benefits/">dead hang benefits guide</a> rates the evidence for each use.</p>

        <h2>Proper Dead Hang Form</h2>
        <p>Grab the bar with an overhand grip at shoulder width. Wrap your thumbs around the bar. Step off the platform and let your full bodyweight hang. Straighten your arms completely.</p>
        <p>Relax your shoulders for a passive hang or pull the shoulder blades down for an active hang. Tuck your pelvis slightly. Bring your legs together. Breathe slowly and do not hold your breath.</p>
        <p>Common mistakes include bent elbows, forced shoulder shrugging, body swinging and breath holding. Each mistake shortens your hold and can make your shoulders and elbows sore.</p>
        <p>The complete <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">step-by-step dead hang instructions</a> cover grip placement, shoulder position, breathing and dismounting. The form guide and the mistakes guide listed under related guides give cues for every body segment and the most common errors.</p>

        <h2 id="workout-plan">Dead Hang Workout Plan</h2>
        <p>A dead hang workout needs three variables: how long you hold, how many sets and how often you train. The table gives a common starting plan. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly, roughly once a week. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>
        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Hold</th><th scope="col">Sets</th><th scope="col">Frequency</th><th scope="col">Rest</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>10 to 20 sec</td><td>2 to 4</td><td>3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days</td><td>60 to 90 sec</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>Add a session only if you stay pain free, and leave at least one rest day between hard sessions. Track total hang time per session alongside your longest hold. Stop a set one or two seconds before your grip gives out and do not train to failure.</p>

        <h3>Full 30-minute session</h3>
        <p>The full session combines hang sets with grip variations and accessory work. You need a secure <a href="/equipment-guide/">pull-up bar</a>, and optionally dumbbells or kettlebells and weight plates. These doses are common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Warm-up, 2 to 3 minutes:</strong> 15 forward and 15 backward arm circles, 10 wrist circles each way per wrist, 15 band pull-aparts, then one or two easy 10 to 15 second hangs. A warm-up helps you feel ready; evidence that it prevents injury is limited. The <a href="/mobility/">mobility guide</a> has a longer routine.</li>
          <li><strong>Block 1, dead hang sets:</strong> 4 sets of an overhand, shoulder-width hold, stopping one or two seconds before your grip gives out, with 90 seconds rest. Record each time. Holds usually fall across sets; for example 25, 20, 17 and 14 seconds (an illustration, not data).</li>
          <li><strong>Block 2, grip variations:</strong> 2 sets of 20 seconds each of an underhand hang (stop if the front of the elbow hurts), a neutral grip hang and an <a href="/deadhang-variations/">active hang</a>, with 60 seconds rest. Keep each at 20 seconds; the aim is exposure to different grip angles, so save your hardest holds for Block 1.</li>
          <li><strong>Block 3, complementary exercises:</strong> 3 sets of 30 second farmer's walks (60 seconds rest), 3 sets of 15 second plate pinches per hand (45 seconds rest) and 2 sets of 15 wrist curls plus 2 sets of 15 reverse wrist curls. The <a href="/complementary-exercises/">complementary exercises guide</a> covers technique.</li>
          <li><strong>Cool-down, 5 minutes:</strong> 30 second forearm flexor and extensor stretches per arm, a 30 second cross-body shoulder stretch per side and a 60 second child's pose.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Weekly schedule</h3>
        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable weekly schedule" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">Session</th><th scope="col">Focus</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Monday</td><td>Heavy</td><td>Full session (Blocks 1 to 3)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>Rest</td><td>Active recovery or light stretching</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>Light</td><td>Block 1 only (4 sets, best controlled hold)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Thursday</td><td>Rest</td><td>Active recovery</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Friday</td><td>Heavy</td><td>Full session (Blocks 1 to 3)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Saturday</td><td>Optional</td><td>Rest, or two easy hangs only</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sunday</td><td>Rest</td><td>Full rest day</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>The <a href="/deadhang-frequency/">dead hang frequency guide</a> explains the spacing. A lighter week about every fourth week is a common coaching convention and not a tested rule: cut total hang time by about half and drop the complementary exercises.</p>

        <h3>Scaling the session</h3>
        <p>If you cannot hold the bar for 10 seconds yet, use a loop band or keep your feet on a box. Record the assist you used so tests stay comparable. Move to full bodyweight hangs at 15 second holds, use one set per variation in Block 2 and skip plate pinches until 20 second hangs are routine. The <a href="/beginner-deadhang/">beginner dead hang guide</a> has the step-by-step path.</p>
        <p>To progress, add weight with a dip belt once you hold a comfortable, pain-free bodyweight hang of about 45 seconds on all four Block 1 sets. Start with 5 to 10 lb (2 to 5 kg), add about 5 lb once every set is clean and stop if your elbows or fingers hurt. The <a href="/deadhang-progressions/">progression ladder</a> and <a href="/training-programs/">training programs</a> cover longer plans.</p>

        <h2 id="muscles-worked">Dead Hang Muscles Worked</h2>
        <p>A dead hang works the forearm finger flexors most. The shoulders, lats, trapezius and trunk assist, and how much depends on whether the hang is passive or active.</p>

        <h3>Primary Muscles</h3>
        <p>Your forearm flexors do the most work. The flexor digitorum profundus, flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor pollicis longus sustain your grip against bodyweight. The forearm has about 20 muscles. The finger flexors do most of the work in a hang.</p>

        <h3>Secondary Muscles</h3>
        <p>In a passive hang the shoulder muscles are mostly relaxed. In an active hang the lower traps, serratus anterior and lats work to hold the shoulder blades down. The posterior deltoids and upper and middle trapezius help keep the shoulder stable. The rhomboids pull the shoulder blades back during active hangs.</p>

        <h3>Stabilizer Muscles</h3>
        <p>Your transverse abdominis and obliques limit lateral sway. The rectus abdominis resists spinal hyperextension. Gluteal muscles steady the pelvis. Deep spinal erectors maintain trunk alignment.</p>

        <p>Learn more about <a href="/grip-strength/">grip strength</a> and <a href="/forearm-exercises/">forearm training</a>.</p>

        <h2>Tracking Your Dead Hang Time</h2>
        <p>Track your holds against your own earlier holds. No validated population norms exist for timed dead hangs, and bodyweight changes what a given hold means. A heavier person with the same forearm strength holds for less time, which is physics and not weakness. Foot support or a loop band takes part of your weight, so record the assist you used.</p>
        <p>Published studies on <a href="/dead-hang-time-by-age/">dead hang times by age</a> give context but no reliable percentiles. The <a href="/deadhang-progressions/">dead hang progression ladder</a> gives the next steps.</p>

        <h2>Dead Hang Variations</h2>
        <p>The main dead hang variations change grip, shoulder position or load to target different training goals.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Passive hang:</strong> Relaxed shoulders for a shoulder and trunk stretch.</li>
          <li><strong>Active hang:</strong> Shoulder blades engaged for shoulder blade control.</li>
          <li><strong>Overhand grip:</strong> The standard grip that matches the pull-up.</li>
          <li><strong>Underhand grip:</strong> An alternative that puts the biceps tendon under stretch; stop if the front of the elbow hurts.</li>
          <li><strong>Mixed grip:</strong> One hand over and one under. The trunk may work to stay still.</li>
          <li><strong>Towel hang:</strong> A harder hold using a towel over the bar.</li>
          <li><strong>One-arm hang:</strong> An advanced hang that puts the full load on one arm.</li>
          <li><strong>Weighted hang:</strong> External load via a dip belt, added only after a comfortable bodyweight hang of about 45 seconds</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The full breakdown is in the <a href="/deadhang-variations/">dead hang variations guide</a>.</p>

        <h2>Training Programs</h2>
        <p>Structured programs give you a week-by-week plan. Three program lengths cover different starting points.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>4-week program:</strong> Builds toward a 30-second hang from a 15 to 20 second baseline; repeat weeks 3 and 4 if you start lower.</li>
          <li><strong>8-week program:</strong> For a comfortable 20 to 30 second hold.</li>
          <li><strong>12-week program:</strong> For a comfortable 30 second or longer hold; adds load or one-arm work as options.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Start with the program that matches your current hold. The <a href="/training-programs/">training programs overview</a> helps you choose.</p>

        <h2>Equipment Recommendations</h2>
        <p>Two bar types cover most home setups. Your choice depends on your living situation, budget and training goals.</p>
        <p>See the <a href="/equipment-guide/">equipment guide</a> for the full overview of bars, chalk and grip aids.</p>

        <h3>Doorway Pull-Up Bars</h3>
        <p>Doorway bars install without screws and fit many door frames. They cost less and need no permanent modification. Check the manufacturer's rated load. Best for renters and beginners.</p>
        <p>See the <a href="/doorway-bars/">doorway bar guide</a> for what to check.</p>

        <h3>Wall-Mounted Pull-Up Bars</h3>
        <p>Wall-mounted bars bolt into studs or masonry for maximum stability. They often have higher load ratings and allow wider grip positions. Installation requires drilling. Best for dedicated home gyms.</p>
        <p>See the <a href="/wall-mounted-bars/">wall-mounted bar guide</a> for what to check.</p>

        <h3>Accessories</h3>
        <p>Chalk dries the skin and increases friction between skin and bar. Grip attachments add training variety with fat grips, towels and pinch blocks.</p>
        <p>See <a href="/equipment-reviews/">how the site assesses equipment</a>. The site has not load-tested any bar.</p>

        <div class="cta-box">
          <h3>Compare Bars Side by Side</h3>
          <p>Not sure whether a doorframe, wall-mounted or ceiling-mounted bar suits your space? The full buying guide compares stability, weight rating and grip diameter across every option.</p>
