        <h2>Safety and Contraindications</h2>
        <p>Dead hangs are suitable for many healthy adults. Start with short holds of 5 to 10 seconds and increase gradually. Step down onto a platform rather than jumping down to protect your joints on dismount.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/injury-prevention/">injury prevention guide</a> covers gradual progression and recovery.</p>

        <h3>Who Should Avoid Dead Hangs</h3>
        <p>Ask a clinician before hanging if any of these apply.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>People with acute disc herniation or spinal instability (get medical clearance first).</li>
          <li>Anyone with an unstable shoulder dislocation or recent shoulder surgery.</li>
          <li>People with unhealed fractures in the hands, wrists, arms or shoulders.</li>
          <li>Anyone who experiences sharp or radiating pain during hanging.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>When to Stop</h3>
        <p>Stop immediately if you feel sharp pain in any joint. Dull muscular fatigue in your forearms is normal. Sharp, shooting or radiating pain is not. Persistent elbow or shoulder pain after dead hangs requires rest and evaluation.</p>
        <p>Read the complete <a href="/deadhang-safety/">dead hang safety guide</a> before starting any program.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>What does a dead hang do?</h3>
          <p>A dead hang trains grip and shoulder endurance. People also use it for shoulder and spine stretching; evidence for those uses is limited. Your forearms, lats, shoulders, traps and core all work during the isometric hold.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How long should you dead hang?</h3>
          <p>A common starting plan is 2 to 4 sets of 10 to 20 seconds with 60 to 90 seconds rest. Train 3 times a week on non-consecutive days. Stop one or two seconds before your grip gives out. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Are dead hangs good for you?</h3>
          <p>A dead hang trains grip and shoulder endurance and suits many fitness levels with the right modification. It is not a complete program. Grip strength is associated with longevity in cohort data. No trial shows that hanging lowers death risk.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Can dead hangs replace pull-ups?</h3>
          <p>Dead hangs cannot replace pull-ups for muscle building. Pull-ups involve concentric and eccentric contractions that drive muscle growth. Dead hangs build the grip and shoulder stability needed to perform pull-ups. Use them as a foundation and not a substitute.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Do dead hangs decompress the spine?</h3>
          <p>Hanging unloads the spine while you hang. No study has measured disc height or pressure during a bodyweight hang. A Cochrane review (Wegner 2013) found traction gives little or no benefit for low back pain. Some people find short hangs ease stiffness.</p>
        </div>

